nt196"},{"accession":"GSM9093571","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient95"},{"accession":"GSM9093543","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient60"},{"accession":"GSM9093526","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient32"},{"accession":"GSM9093738","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient348"},{"accession":"GSM9093529","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient37"},{"accession":"GSM9093741","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient351"},{"accession":"GSM9093658","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient237"},{"accession":"GSM9093632","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient205"},{"accession":"GSM9093735","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient343"},{"accession":"GSM9093532","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient41"},{"accession":"GSM9093635","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient209"},{"accession":"GSM9093552","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient73"},{"accession":"GSM9093500","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient3"},{"accession":"GSM9093712","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient317"},{"accession":"GSM9093629","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient200"},{"accession":"GSM9093603","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient150"},{"accession":"GSM9093789","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient406"},{"accession":"GSM9093706","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient311"},{"accession":"GSM9093523","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient28"},{"accession":"GSM9093606","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient158"},{"accession":"GSM9093709","title":"HNSCC_FFPE_patient314"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":303,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE301nnn/GSE301983/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1287988"},"200286464":{"uid":"200286464","accession":"GSE286464","gds":"","title":"Dietary serine restriction reduces head and neck cancer cell plasticity through epigenetic changes in the chromatin landscape [RNA-seq]","summary":"Head and neck cancer with its major subsite, oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC), is a devastating malignancy with limited treatment options. Given that recent advances in nutritional interventions have been recognized as potential strategies for targeting cancer, we investigated the role of dietary serine restriction in OSCC cell fate decisions. We now report that human OSCC cells behave like serine auxotrophs, depending on exogenous serine to promote growth and sustain plastic cell states, such as cancer stem cells and cells with EMT features. We show that restriction of serine induces endogenous serine synthesis and generates alpha-ketoglutarate (aKG), a co-substrate for JMJD3 nuclear dioxygenase, which demethylates H3K27me3 and de-represses differentiation genes.  This is balanced by the loss of H3K4me3 and inhibition of stemness genes. Likewise, dietary serine restriction inhibits orthotopic OSCC growth in syngeneic mice. Our work provides the basis for including precision nutrition to advance treatments for OSCC patients.","gpl":"30173","gse":"286464","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/09/02","suppfile":"RESULTS","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8728155","title":"Cal27_Complete_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8728158","title":"Cal27_SS_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8728161","title":"HSC3_Complete_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8728164","title":"HSC_SS_rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8728163","title":"HSC3_Complete_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8728157","title":"Cal27_Complete_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8728160","title":"Cal27_SS_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8728162","title":"HSC3_Complete_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8728165","title":"HSC_SS_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8728166","title":"HSC_SS_rep3"},{"accession":"GSM8728156","title":"Cal27_Complete_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8728159","title":"Cal27_SS_rep2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["41279982"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE286nnn/GSE286464/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1209546"},"200344708":{"uid":"200344708","accession":"GSE344708","gds":"","title":"Comprehensive epigenomic profiling reveals diagnostic and prognostic  biomarkers in oral squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Objective: The number of patients with oral cancer is increasing, and despite advances in diagnostic techniques  and the development of novel therapies, survival rates have not significantly improved. Therefore, innovative  approaches that serve as potential therapeutic targets and diagnostic markers are required. The epigenome plays  a critical role in maintaining cellular homeostasis and regulating the expression of genes associated with cancer.  Among epigenetic mechanisms, super-enhancers (SEs) are key regulatory regions that drive gene expression.  However, the epigenomic landscape of oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) remains relatively underexplored  compared with that of other carcinomas.  Methods: In this study, we performed ChIP-seq analysis of histone H3 lysine 27 acetylation (H3K27ac) in OSCC  cell lines to identify SE regions. The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) datasets of head and neck cancer (HNC) were  analyzed to examine the clinical samples.  Results: We detected several coding genes and non-coding RNAs that were potentially regulated by SEs. Fur-  thermore, we identified SE-controlled genomic regions that are specific to OSCC by integrating genome-wide  expression analyses. Analysis of the TCGA dataset revealed that several of these genes were upregulated in tu-  mors and were associated with the prognosis of patients.  Conclusions: Collectively, our findings demonstrate that ChIP-seq analysis combined with public database valida-  tion provides a novel strategy for identifying potential diagnostic markers in OSCC.","gpl":"17077","gse":"344708","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by array","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/09/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9983532","title":"SAS JQ1"},{"accession":"GSM9983531","title":"SAS vehicle"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":2,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE344nnn/GSE344708/","geo2r":"yes","bioproject":"PRJNA1516581"},"200339480":{"uid":"200339480","accession":"GSE339480","gds":"","title":"An advanced scRNA-seq-compatible lentiviral barcoding system resolves treatment-associated clonal selection and transcriptional states in HNSCC cells","summary":"Cancer therapy response is shaped by pre-existing heterogeneity and treatment-associated selection within tumor cell populations. In this study, we applied a single-cell RNA-seq-compatible lentiviral BC24 barcode system to FaDu hypopharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma cells to link clonal identity with transcriptional cell state during cytotoxic treatment response. Barcoded FaDu cells were profiled by 10x Genomics single-cell RNA sequencing together with targeted recovery of expressed BC24 lineage barcodes from amplified cDNA. The dataset enables analysis of untreated and treatment-selected FaDu cells, including cisplatin- and irradiation-associated transcriptional remodeling, barcode-defined clonal enrichment, and the relationship between therapy-associated clonal selection and pre-existing epithelial cell states.","gpl":"34281","gse":"339480","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/31","suppfile":"CSV, TAR","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9896250","title":"treatment-induced clonal selection (control, cisplatin, irradiation)"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":1,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE339nnn/GSE339480/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1499019"},"200235459":{"uid":"200235459","accession":"GSE235459","gds":"","title":"p63 and p73 cooperate to promote cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma through largely overlapping transcriptional programs [RNA-Seq]","summary":"Aberrant expression of transcriptional regulators can affect oncogenic gene expression programs in cancer. Mutations in the tumor suppressor p53 are commonly found in UV-damaged skin and protect damaged epidermal cells from senescence and or oncogene-induced apoptosis, favoring cancer formation. In contrast, the other p53 family members p63 and p73 are rarely mutated in cancer and p63 is often amplified or overexpressed in lung, esophagus, and head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (SCC). Here, we demonstrate that both p63 and p73 are required for cell proliferation in epidermal cells and in skin SCC and are overexpressed in preneoplastic lesions and in skin SCCs. p63/p73 form heterotetramers and co-occupy thousands of regulatory regions, jointly controlling a transcriptional program that promotes cell proliferation and tumorigenesis. The combination of gene knockdown with transcriptomic and epigenetic analyses revealed that p63 and p73 control a transcriptional feed-forward circuit that sustains cell proliferation. We find that in skin SCC a key signaling pathway downstream of p63/p73 is the Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR)/MAP kinase. p63/p73 directly and positively control transcription of the EGFR ligands, among which amphiregulin (AREG) is the most highly expressed in skin SCC. Like p63/p73, AREG is critical to maintain skin SCC proliferative potential, anchorage independent growth, and to promote tumorigenesis. Thus, p63 and p73 act as oncogenic drivers in skin SCC, with AREG being a crucial non-cell-autonomous effector downstream of p63 and p73 in skin SCC formation.","gpl":"20301","gse":"235459","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/28","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM7502597","title":"RNAseq sip73 exp2"},{"accession":"GSM7502589","title":"RNAseq siCTRL exp2"},{"accession":"GSM7502592","title":"RNAseq sip63 exp1"},{"accession":"GSM7502595","title":"RNAseq sip63 exp4"},{"accession":"GSM7502598","title":"RNAseq sip73 exp3"},{"accession":"GSM7502590","title":"RNAseq siCTRL exp3"},{"accession":"GSM7502599","title":"RNAseq sip73 exp4"},{"accession":"GSM7502593","title":"RNAseq sip63 exp2"},{"accession":"GSM7502596","title":"RNAseq sip73 exp1"},{"accession":"GSM7502591","title":"RNAseq siCTRL exp4"},{"accession":"GSM7502594","title":"RNAseq sip63 exp3"},{"accession":"GSM7502588","title":"RNAseq siCTRL exp1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE235nnn/GSE235459/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA986095"},"200297537":{"uid":"200297537","accession":"GSE297537","gds":"","title":"Single-Cell Transcriptomic Landscape of Laryngeal Squamous Cell Carcinoma from Precancerous to Cancerous Lesions","summary":"Laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma (LSCC), a significant and aggressive type of head and neck cancer, primarily originates from the precancerous lesions of the larynx. However, the mechanisms driving the progression from laryngeal precancerosis to carcinoma remain unclear. To address this and provide therapeutic candidates, we compile a single-cell transcriptome atlas of the laryngeal precancerous lesion, carcinoma in situ, and squamous cell carcinoma from patient samples. We uncover the complex cellular landscape of these lesions, revealing distinct cell populations and their unique transcriptional profiles at various stages of disease progression. Notably, our analysis identifies a key subtype of epithelial cells in laryngeal precancerosis, characterized by the expression of TOP2A, HMGB2 and PTTG1, linked to aggressive LSCC behavior. Within this TOP2A+HMGB2+PTTG1+subset, polo-like kinase-1 (PLK1) emerged as a pivotal therapeutic target. Further studies demonstrate that a component derived from the Chinese Medicine Compound ReYanNingHeJi, quercetin, effectively target and inhibit PLK1 activity, thereby reducing cellular proliferation. Therefore, this study sheds new light on the understanding of precancerous lesions progression and offers potential candidates for clinical intervention.","gpl":"24676","gse":"297537","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/25","suppfile":"MTX, TSV","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8994144","title":"sample 6 laryngeal squamous cell"},{"accession":"GSM8994141","title":"sample 3 carcinoma in situ"},{"accession":"GSM8994139","title":"sample 1 high-grade epithelial hyperplasia"},{"accession":"GSM8994142","title":"sample 4 carcinoma in situ"},{"accession":"GSM8994140","title":"sample 2 high-grade epithelial hyperplasia"},{"accession":"GSM8994143","title":"sample 5 laryngeal squamous cell"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE297nnn/GSE297537/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1265160"},"200255118":{"uid":"200255118","accession":"GSE255118","gds":"","title":"Single-cell RNA sequencing from immune cells in oral cancer tumors","summary":"This data is mainly used to investigate the tumor micro-environment of head and neck cancer. A total of 9,894 immune cells were isolated from 8 oral tumor tissues with OSCC patients by analyzing single-cell RNA seq.","gpl":"24676","gse":"255118","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/17","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8064458","title":"L010T, seRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064457","title":"L009T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064463","title":"L020T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064456","title":"L007T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064462","title":"L017T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064459","title":"L011T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064460","title":"L013T, scRNAseq"},{"accession":"GSM8064461","title":"L016T, scRNAseq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":8,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["42271351"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE255nnn/GSE255118/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1073548"},"200338805":{"uid":"200338805","accession":"GSE338805","gds":"","title":"α7 integrin is a critical co-regulator of chromatin accessibility and gene expression in HPV-negative head and neck cancer","summary":"Epigenetic regulation and gene transcription are dynamically modulated by extracellular signals, including focal adhesions; however, the role of integrins in these processes remains poorly understood. In this study in HPV-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma, we combined investigations in physiological 3-dimensional matrix cell models with multi-omics analyses, bioinformatics and artificial intelligence, to unmask the integrin effector signaling network that reshapes chromatin to drive cancer. Among the 12 α/β1 integrin receptors, α7 integrin emerged as the most potent modulator of histone post-translational modifications, associated with significant changes in gene transcription and chromatin accessibility. Mechanistically, phosphoproteomics revealed significant inhibition of MAPK- and PI3K/AKT-dependent signaling with concomitant functional impairment of cell cycling and cell survival upon ITGA7 depletion. In summary, these findings establish α7 integrin as a key co-regulator of chromatin accessibility and gene transcription in HPV-negative HNSCC, acting through an AURKA-centered axis that couples adhesion signaling to cell cycle control and patient survival, and nominate AURKA as an actionable therapeutic node downstream of the currently non-druggable ITGA7.","gpl":"24676","gse":"338805","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/01","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9881914","title":"SAS cells, siControl, biological replicate 2 (R-20)"},{"accession":"GSM9881917","title":"Cal33 cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 1 (R-9)"},{"accession":"GSM9881911","title":"Cal33 cells, siControl, biological replicate 3 (R-5)"},{"accession":"GSM9881912","title":"Cal33 cells, siControl, biological replicate 4 (R-6)"},{"accession":"GSM9881923","title":"SAS cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 3 (R-27)"},{"accession":"GSM9881909","title":"Cal33 cells, siControl, biological replicate 1 (R-3)"},{"accession":"GSM9881920","title":"Cal33 cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 4 (R-12)"},{"accession":"GSM9881924","title":"SAS cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 4 (R-28)"},{"accession":"GSM9881921","title":"SAS cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 1 (R-25)"},{"accession":"GSM9881915","title":"SAS cells, siControl, biological replicate 3 (R-21)"},{"accession":"GSM9881918","title":"Cal33 cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 2 (R-10)"},{"accession":"GSM9881913","title":"SAS cells, siControl, biological replicate 1 (R-19)"},{"accession":"GSM9881910","title":"Cal33 cells, siControl, biological replicate 2 (R-4)"},{"accession":"GSM9881922","title":"SAS cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 2 (R-26)"},{"accession":"GSM9881916","title":"SAS cells, siControl, biological replicate 4 (R-22)"},{"accession":"GSM9881919","title":"Cal33 cells, siITGA7, biological replicate 3 (R-11)"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":16,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE338nnn/GSE338805/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1496062"},"200282433":{"uid":"200282433","accession":"GSE282433","gds":"","title":"Microgravity suppresses oral squamous cell carcinoma growth through Piezo1-mediated regulation of calcium ion homeostasis and the autophagy pathway","summary":"Background: Oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) is the most common form of oral cancer, with metastasis significantly contributing to its poor prognosis. Emerging research suggests that microgravity may exert anti-tumor effects by inducing cell death, although the precise mechanisms remain unclear. Methods: Rotary Cell Culture System (RCCS) was used to simulate microgravity, allowing observation of its effects on the proliferation and migration abilities of tumor cells. Key genes and pathways influenced by simulated microgravity were identified using RNA sequencing (RNA-seq). In the tongue load-bearing tumor mouse model, we utilized a hindlimb unloading (HU) method to simulate microgravity and investigate its impact on tumor growth, as well as to validate the involvement of identified key genes and pathways. Results: Simulated microgravity induced significant morphological changes in OSCC cells, leading to the formation of multicellular spheroids and altering their biological behavior. RNA sequencing identified PIEZO1 as a key mechanosensitive gene upregulated under microgravity, along with the activation of autophagy-related pathways. In vivo experiments using a tongue load-bearing mouse model demonstrated that simulated microgravity suppressed tumor growth, which was associated with enhanced autophagy and disrupted calcium homeostasis. Piezo1 inhibition partially reversed these effects, confirming its critical role in microgravity-induced OSCC suppression. Conclusion: Simulated microgravity suppresses OSCC growth through Piezo1-mediated calcium dysregulation and enhanced mitophagy. Inhibiting Piezo1 reverses these effects, suggesting its potential as a therapeutic target. This study highlights microgravity-based approaches as promising strategies for OSCC treatment.","gpl":"24676","gse":"282433","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/08/01","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8643069","title":"HSC-3 cells, Microgravity (RCCS), biol rep2"},{"accession":"GSM8643066","title":"HSC-3 cells, Normal Gravity, biol rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM8643065","title":"HSC-3 cells, Normal Gravity, biol rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM8643068","title":"HSC-3 cells, Microgravity (RCCS), biol rep1"},{"accession":"GSM8643067","title":"HSC-3 cells, Normal Gravity, biol rep 3"},{"accession":"GSM8643070","title":"HSC-3 cells, Microgravity (RCCS), biol rep3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE282nnn/GSE282433/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1188573"},"200333740":{"uid":"200333740","accession":"GSE333740","gds":"","title":"Human HN-SCC-151 HPV-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma cells treated with DMSO or the SUV420H1 inhibitor A-196/siNC (control) or siSUV420H1 siRNAs.","summary":"This SuperSeries is composed of the SubSeries listed below.","gpl":"30172;30173","gse":"333740","taxon":"Mus musculus; Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Genome binding/occupancy profiling by high throughput sequencing; Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/07/31","suppfile":"BED, BIGWIG","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9772006","title":"VS917_SCC151_si1SUV420H1_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9771986","title":"VS1011_SCC151_A-196_H4K20me3_rep2"},{"accession":"GSM9772003","title":"VS914_SCC151_siNC_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9771983","title":"VS1005_SCC151_DMSO_H4K20me3_rep2"},{"accession":