2","title":"VS913_SCC151_siNC_rep2_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772005","title":"VS916_SCC151_si1SUV420H1_rep2_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772008","title":"VS1045_SCC151_DMSO_rep2_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772011","title":"VS1048_SCC151_SUV420H1_inhibitor_rep2_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772014","title":"VS1753_MOC1_Tumor_Suv-WT_S87_rep2_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772003","title":"VS914_SCC151_siNC_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772017","title":"VS1756_MOC1_Tumor_Suv-WT_S92_rep5_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772006","title":"VS917_SCC151_si1SUV420H1_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772020","title":"VS1760_MOC1_Tumor_mSuvKO1_S97_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772012","title":"VS1049_SCC151_SUV420H1_inhibitor_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772009","title":"VS1046_SCC151_DMSO_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772015","title":"VS1754_MOC1_Tumor_Suv-WT_S88_rep3_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772018","title":"VS1757_MOC1_Tumor_mSuvKO1_S93_rep1_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772021","title":"VS1761_MOC1_Tumor_mSuvKO1_S99_rep4_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772004","title":"VS915_SCC151_si1SUV420H1_rep1_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772007","title":"VS1044_SCC151_DMSO_rep1_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772013","title":"VS1752_MOC1_Tumor_Suv-WT_S86_rep1_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772016","title":"VS1755_MOC1_Tumor_Suv-WT_S89_rep4_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772001","title":"VS912_SCC151_siNC_rep1_rnaseq"},{"accession":"GSM9772010","title":"VS1047_SCC151_SUV420H1_inhibitor_rep1_rnaseq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":21,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE333nnn/GSE333739/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1472478"},"200324492":{"uid":"200324492","accession":"GSE324492","gds":"","title":"Combination AURKA and WEE1 inhibition exhibits efficacy in EGFR or pan-ERBB inhibitor resistant head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Over 600,000 cases of head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) are diagnosed globally each year. Many HNSCCs overexpress the ERBB family member EGFR, and EGFR inhibitors (EGFRis) and pan-ERBB inhibitors (ERBBis) are clinically active in treatment of locally advanced, metastatic or recurrent HNSCC. However, resistance to these inhibitors typically develops, often associated with epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT). Aurora kinase A (AURKA), a mitotic regulator with expanded signaling functions in tumors, has been reported to reverse EGFR resistance in EGFR-mutated lung cancer. To identify strategies to overcome resistance in HNSCC, we developed HNSCC cell models that were treatment-naïve parental, or selected for resistance to the EGFRi erlotinib, or the ERBBi afatinib. The resistant HNSCC models had typically undergone partial EMT, consistent with clinical resistance, associated with upregulation of the AURKA partner protein NEDD9. Synergy of the AURKA inhibitor VIC-1911 with erlotinib or afatinib in parental models was reduced in resistant models in short term growth assays. In longer term clonogenic assays and in vivo, cells selected for EGFRi/ERBBi resistance showed heightened sensitivity to VIC-1911, contributing to reduced synergy. Based on increased AURKA dependence, we compared combination of VIC-1911 with adavosertib, an inhibitor of the cell cycle checkpoint regulator WEE1, in parental versus resistant models. These showed a combination effect both in vitro and in vivo, that was retained in ERBBi-resistant xenografts, suggesting the potential value of combined AURKA and WEE1 inhibitor use in patients with ERBB inhibitor-resistant HNSCC.","gpl":"16791","gse":"324492","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/07/28","suppfile":"CSV, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9578622","title":"Cal27 erlotinib resistant, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578620","title":"Cal27 afatinib resistant, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578614","title":"Fadu afatinib resistant, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578617","title":"Fadu erlotinib resistant, bio rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM9578623","title":"Cal27 erlotinib resistant, bio rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM9578612","title":"Fadu parental, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578618","title":"Cal27 parental, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578615","title":"Fadu afatinib resistant, bio rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM9578621","title":"Cal27 afatinib resistant, bio rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM9578619","title":"Cal27 parental, bio rep 2"},{"accession":"GSM9578616","title":"Fadu erlotinib resistant, bio rep 1"},{"accession":"GSM9578613","title":"Fadu parental, bio rep 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["42531323"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE324nnn/GSE324492/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1435450"},"200325366":{"uid":"200325366","accession":"GSE325366","gds":"","title":"A positive feedback loop between BACH1 and IL-1β promotes the progression of HPV-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma","summary":"Human papillomavirus (HPV)-negative head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) is associated with a poorer prognosis compared to its HPV-positive counterpart; however, the underlying regulatory molecular mechanisms driving its progression remain poorly understood. In this study, we identify BTB Domain and CNC Homolog 1 (BACH1) as a critical oncogenic regulator that is upregulated in HPV-negative HNSCC. We demonstrate that BACH1 promotes tumor progression by enhancing the proliferation and inhibiting apoptosis of cancer cells. Mechanistically, BACH1 is associated with the SWI/SNF chromatin remodeling complex and transcriptionally activates downstream target genes, including IL1B, thereby inducing a transcriptional reprogramming that promotes proliferation and resists apoptosis. Notably, BACH1-driven upregulation of interleukin-1β (IL-1β) establishes a positive feedback loop that sustains oncogenic signaling. Pharmacological disruption of this axis using the IL-1 receptor antagonist Anakinra significantly attenuates tumor growth in vitro and in vivo. Clinically, co-upregulation of BACH1, BRG1, and IL-1β is correlated with reduced overall survival in patients with HPV-negative HNSCC. Collectively, our findings characterize the BACH1–IL-1β signaling axis as a prognostic biomarker and highlight IL-1R blockade as a promising therapeutic strategy for the treatment of HPV-negative HNSCC.","gpl":"24676","gse":"325366","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/07/22","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9601920","title":"BACH_3"},{"accession":"GSM9601917","title":"NC_3"},{"accession":"GSM9601923","title":"NC_6"},{"accession":"GSM9601926","title":"IL1B_3"},{"accession":"GSM9601915","title":"NC_1"},{"accession":"GSM9601921","title":"NC_4"},{"accession":"GSM9601918","title":"BACH_1"},{"accession":"GSM9601924","title":"IL1B_1"},{"accession":"GSM9601919","title":"BACH_2"},{"accession":"GSM9601916","title":"NC_2"},{"accession":"GSM9601922","title":"NC_5"},{"accession":"GSM9601925","title":"IL1B_2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":12,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["42178570"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE325nnn/GSE325366/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1439794"},"200311744":{"uid":"200311744","accession":"GSE311744","gds":"","title":"RNA sequencing analysis of transcriptomic changes in human umbilical vein endothelial cells co-cultured with exosomes derived from GLS1-knockdown head and neck cancer cells","summary":"The aim of this study is to investigate the influence of exosomes derived from GLS1-knockdown head and neck cancer cells on the transcriptomic profile of human umbilical vein endothelial cells","gpl":"34284","gse":"311744","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/07/15","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9331297","title":"HUVEC_ co-cultured shGFP_2"},{"accession":"GSM9331298","title":"HUVEC_ co-cultured shGLS1_1"},{"accession":"GSM9331299","title":"HUVEC_ co-cultured shGLS1_2"},{"accession":"GSM9331296","title":"HUVEC_co-cultured shGFP_1"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":4,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["42070227"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE311nnn/GSE311744/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1370859"},"200319374":{"uid":"200319374","accession":"GSE319374","gds":"","title":"RNA-seq profiling of post-hypoxic head and neck cancer cell lines","summary":"Hypoxia is an important cause of radiotherapy resistance in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC). To investigate the transcriptional expression associated with post-hypoxic survival, we performed RNA-seq profiling of HPV-negative (FADU) and HPV-positive (SCC154) hypoxia-fate-mapped cell lines, hereafter called FADU-hfm and SCC154-hfm. Non-hypoxic (DsRed-positive) and post-hypoxic (GFP-positive) cells were sorted from 3D hfm-spheroids using fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS) and analyzed under normoxic conditions. The resulting dataset provides gene expression profiles of non-hypoxic and post-hypoxic cells.","gpl":"30173","gse":"319374","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/07/01","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9516812","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative post-hypoxic replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM9516815","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative post-hypoxic replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM9516821","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive post-hypoxic replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM9516818","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative non-hypoxic replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM9516824","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive non-hypoxic replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM9516820","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive non-hypoxic replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM9516823","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative post-hypoxic replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM9516817","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive post-hypoxic replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM9516826","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative post-hypoxic replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM9516822","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative non-hypoxic replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM9516825","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive post-hypoxic replicate 4"},{"accession":"GSM9516828","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive post-hypoxic replicate 5"},{"accession":"GSM9516811","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative non-hypoxic replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM9516814","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative non-hypoxic replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM9516813","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive non-hypoxic replicate 1"},{"accession":"GSM9516816","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive non-hypoxic replicate 2"},{"accession":"GSM9516819","title":"FADU-hfm HPV-negative post-hypoxic replicate 3"},{"accession":"GSM9516827","title":"SCC154-hfm HPV-positive non-hypoxic replicate 5"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":18,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["41972846"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE319nnn/GSE319374/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1423175"},"200320559":{"uid":"200320559","accession":"GSE320559","gds":"","title":"LUCAT1 Drives Arecoline-Induced Head and Neck Cancer Progression via STAT1-Mediated Transcriptional Regulation","summary":"Background: Areca nut chewing is a major environmental risk factor for head and neck cancer (HNC), particularly in Southeast Asia. However, the molecular mechanisms that link areca nut exposure to malignant progression remain poorly understood. Long noncoding RNAs (lncRNAs) have emerged as critical regulators of oncogenesis, but their role in areca nut-associated HNC remains unexplored. Methods: We performed functional assays, transcriptomic profiling, and bioinformatic analyses to investigate the role of the lncRNA LUCAT1 in arecoline-treated HNC cells. Cell motility, epithelial–mesenchymal transition (EMT), reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels, and therapeutic resistance were assessed following LUCAT1 knockdown or overexpression. We identified upstream regulators of LUCAT1 through promoter analysis, transcription factor knockdown, and pharmacological inhibition. Results: LUCAT1 expression was significantly upregulated by arecoline exposure and promoted cell motility, EMT, ROS clearance, and resistance to radiotherapy and chemotherapy. Knockdown of LUCAT1 reversed these malignant phenotypes and suppressed antioxidant enzyme expression, partly through modulation of the p38 MAPK pathway. Transcriptomic and promoter analyses identified STAT1 as a key transcription factor activated by arecoline through muscarinic acetylcholine receptor (mAChR) signaling. Functional rescue experiments confirmed that LUCAT1 acts downstream of STAT1 to sustain arecoline-induced tumor aggressiveness. Conclusion: Our findings define a novel mAChR–STAT1–LUCAT1 regulatory axis that mediates areca nut-induced malignant progression in HNC. This study not only reveals a critical molecular pathway linking environmental carcinogen exposure to oncogenic transcriptional reprogramming but also highlights LUCAT1 as a promising target for therapeutic intervention in high-risk HNC patients.","gpl":"18573","gse":"320559","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/06/25","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"G