, Biological Replicate A"},{"accession":"GSM9481435","title":"Patient HNC0083, PDX sample"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":114,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE317nnn/GSE317901/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1415770"},"200316533":{"uid":"200316533","accession":"GSE316533","gds":"","title":"Loss of JAK1 Function Causes G2/M Cell Cycle Defects Vulnerable to KIF18A Inhibition [Detroit562 CRISPR Screen]","summary":"Therapeutic resistance to DNA damage is a significant challenge in oncology. To gain insight into biological mechanisms that cause DNA damage resistance and to inform strategies for achieving synergy with therapeutic radiation, we performed parallel pooled genetic CRISPR-Cas9 screening for survival in high-risk head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) subtypes. Surprisingly, in addition to known mediators of radiotherapy response, including ATM, DNAPK, and NF-κB signaling, loss of JAK1 was identified as a driver of tumor cell radioresistance. Knockout of JAK1 in HNSCC increased cell survival by enhancing the DNA damage-dependent G2/M cell cycle arrest and delaying progression to radiation-induced mitotic catastrophe. In line with this finding, both JAK1 knockout and kinase inhibition with abrocitinib prevented subsequent formation of radiation-induced micronuclei. Loss of JAK1 function did not affect canonical CDK1 signaling but instead reduced activation of PLK1 and AURKA, two kinases with auxiliary roles in the regulation of G2 and M phase progression. Correspondingly, using both EdU labelling and live cell imaging techniques, JAK1 loss was found to cause prolonged metaphase, mitotic slippage, and progression to tetraploidy. Targeting the mitotic kinesin KIF18A with the small molecule sovilnesib exacerbated mitotic stress and enhanced the efficacy of radiation. These studies establish KIF18A inhibition as a strategy to counteract the protective G2/M cell cycle arrest induced by DNA damage and to thus enhance tumor cell sensitivity to radiation therapy.","gpl":"34281","gse":"316533","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/01/21","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9455477","title":"Detroit562, control treatment, sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM9455476","title":"Detroit562, control treatment, sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM9455479","title":"Detroit562, radiation, sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM9455478","title":"Detroit562, radiation, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9455475","title":"Detroit562, control treatment, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9455480","title":"Detroit562, radiation, sample 3"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40060568"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE316nnn/GSE316533/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1403275"},"200316529":{"uid":"200316529","accession":"GSE316529","gds":"","title":"Loss of JAK1 Function Causes G2/M Cell Cycle Defects Vulnerable to KIF18A Inhibition [Detroit562 RNAseq]","summary":"Therapeutic resistance to DNA damage is a significant challenge in oncology. To gain insight into biological mechanisms that cause DNA damage resistance and to inform strategies for achieving synergy with therapeutic radiation, we performed parallel pooled genetic CRISPR-Cas9 screening for survival in high-risk head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) subtypes. Surprisingly, in addition to known mediators of radiotherapy response, including ATM, DNAPK, and NF-κB signaling, loss of JAK1 was identified as a driver of tumor cell radioresistance. Knockout of JAK1 in HNSCC increased cell survival by enhancing the DNA damage-dependent G2/M cell cycle arrest and delaying progression to radiation-induced mitotic catastrophe. In line with this finding, both JAK1 knockout and kinase inhibition with abrocitinib prevented subsequent formation of radiation-induced micronuclei. Loss of JAK1 function did not affect canonical CDK1 signaling but instead reduced activation of PLK1 and AURKA, two kinases with auxiliary roles in the regulation of G2 and M phase progression. Correspondingly, using both EdU labelling and live cell imaging techniques, JAK1 loss was found to cause prolonged metaphase, mitotic slippage, and progression to tetraploidy. Targeting the mitotic kinesin KIF18A with the small molecule sovilnesib exacerbated mitotic stress and enhanced the efficacy of radiation. These studies establish KIF18A inhibition as a strategy to counteract the protective G2/M cell cycle arrest induced by DNA damage and to thus enhance tumor cell sensitivity to radiation therapy.","gpl":"34281","gse":"316529","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/01/21","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9455443","title":"Detroit562, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9455446","title":"Detroit562, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9455448","title":"Detroit562, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM9455445","title":"Detroit562, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM9455444","title":"Detroit562, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM9455447","title":"Detroit562, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40060568"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE316nnn/GSE316529/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1403280"},"200314601":{"uid":"200314601","accession":"GSE314601","gds":"","title":"Loss of JAK1 Function Causes G2/M Cell Cycle Defects Vulnerable to KIF18A Inhibition","summary":"Therapeutic resistance to DNA damage is a significant challenge in oncology. To gain insight into biological mechanisms that cause DNA damage resistance and to inform strategies for achieving synergy with therapeutic radiation, we performed parallel pooled genetic CRISPR-Cas9 screening for survival in high-risk head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) subtypes. Surprisingly, in addition to known mediators of radiotherapy response, including ATM, DNAPK, and NF-κB signaling, loss of JAK1 was identified as a driver of tumor cell radioresistance. Knockout of JAK1 in HNSCC increased cell survival by enhancing the DNA damage-dependent G2/M cell cycle arrest and delaying progression to radiation-induced mitotic catastrophe. In line with this finding, both JAK1 knockout and kinase inhibition with abrocitinib prevented subsequent formation of radiation-induced micronuclei. Loss of JAK1 function did not affect canonical CDK1 signaling but instead reduced activation of PLK1 and AURKA, two kinases with auxiliary roles in the regulation of G2 and M phase progression. Correspondingly, using both EdU labelling and live cell imaging techniques, JAK1 loss was found to cause prolonged metaphase, mitotic slippage, and progression to tetraploidy. Targeting the mitotic kinesin KIF18A with the small molecule sovilnesib exacerbated mitotic stress and enhanced the efficacy of radiation. These studies establish KIF18A inhibition as a strategy to counteract the protective G2/M cell cycle arrest induced by DNA damage and to thus enhance tumor cell sensitivity to radiation therapy.","gpl":"34281","gse":"314601","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/01/21","suppfile":"XLSX","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9405370","title":"Cal27, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM9405373","title":"Cal27, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 3"},{"accession":"GSM9405372","title":"Cal27, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 2"},{"accession":"GSM9405371","title":"Cal27, JAK1 knockout gRNA, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9405368","title":"Cal27, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 1"},{"accession":"GSM9405369","title":"Cal27, non-targeting control gRNA, sample 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40060568"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE314nnn/GSE314601/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1392191"},"200302640":{"uid":"200302640","accession":"GSE302640","gds":"","title":"Cornulin transcriptomic analysis in Cal27 oral cancer cells","summary":"RNA-Seq profiling of Cal27 Oral squamous carcinoma cells overexpressing Cornulin (CRNN), GFP control, and untreated group (UNT) to study differential gene expression. This project investigates the transcriptomic alterations induced by the overexpression of Cornulin (CRNN) in the Cal27 human oral squamous cell carcinoma cell line. The study design included three experimental groups: (1) Cornulin-overexpressing Cal27 cells, (2) GFP-expressing Cal27 cells as vector controls, and (3) untreated Cal27 cells. Total RNA was extracted using Trizol reagent, followed by RNA quality assessment via Bioanalyzer. Strand-specific mRNA libraries were prepared using the TruSeq Stranded mRNA kit, pooled at 6 nM, and sequenced using an Illumina NovaSeq 6000 S4 flow cell in a paired-end (2x100 bp) configuration. Raw FASTQ files were analyzed by aligning to the human genome (GRCh38), and gene-level quantification was performed. Differential expression analysis was conducted using the DESeq2 R package. DEG lists from each comparison group were subjected to pre-ranked gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) to identify enriched pathways and gene ontologies. The dataset provides insights into Cornulin-mediated gene regulation.","gpl":"24676","gse":"302640","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2026/01/12","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9107827","title":"CRNN2"},{"accession":"GSM9107830","title":"UNT1"},{"accession":"GSM9107826","title":"CRNN1"},{"accession":"GSM9107829","title":"GFP2"},{"accession":"GSM9107828","title":"GFP1"},{"accession":"GSM9107831","title":"UNT2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["41145788"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE302nnn/GSE302640/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1291633"},"200315646":{"uid":"200315646","accession":"GSE315646","gds":"","title":"Humanized and Charge-Optimized CSPG4-Specific CAR-T Cells Show Enhanced Efficacy Against Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma","summary":"Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T cell therapy for head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) is hampered by the lack of a robust tumor antigen and a suitable CAR design.CSPG4 is highly expressed in HNSCC clinical samples and cell lines, correlating with poor prognosis. Its knockout markedly reduced tumor cell proliferation in vitro and in vivo, validating CSPG4 as an attractive CAR target. First‑generation CSPG4.CAR‑T cells bearing the murine scFv 763.74 showed potent in vitro cytotoxicity but failed to control tumor growth in vivo due to excessive tonic signaling and early exhaustion. By humanizing the scFv framework to reduce positive_x001E_charge residues and immunogenicity, we generated CAR_x001E_T cells with low tonic signaling, diminished exhaustion and differentiation, enhanced tumor_x001E_specific killing, and prolonged in vivo persistence, resulting in superior antitumor activity in both xenograft and PDX models.","gpl":"24676;16791","gse":"315646","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput se