8/18","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM6533012","title":"RNA-seq-shA1-3"},{"accession":"GSM6533009","title":"RNA-seq-mock"},{"accession":"GSM6533013","title":"shA1-2"},{"accession":"GSM6533010","title":"shcon"},{"accession":"GSM6533011","title":"shA1-3"},{"accession":"GSM6533008","title":"shmock"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":6,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":[],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE212nnn/GSE212451/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA875448"},"200297506":{"uid":"200297506","accession":"GSE297506","gds":"","title":"Chronic ISG15 Exposure Accelerates CD8 T Cell Dysfunction while Increasing PD-1 Blockade Sensitivity in Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma","summary":"Immunotherapy has emerged as a promising treatment for head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), but clinical responses remain limited. Elevated expression of interferon-stimulated gene 15 (ISG15), associated with oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC), may contribute to this limited efficacy. While chronic interferon signaling impairs CD8 T cell function, the specific role of secreted ISG15 in T cell exhaustion remains unclear. Analyzing human OSCC datasets, we observed significant enrichment of the ISG core score, including ISG15, in tumors compared to adjacent non-tumor tissues. Using a novel in vitro model of human T cell dysfunction, we found that acute ISG15 exposure enhances CD8 T cell effector functions, whereas prolonged ISG15 exposure induces severe dysfunction via an endocytic mechanism independent of LFA-1 signaling. In an immunocompetent orthotopic OSCC model, ISG15-expressing tumors exhibited accelerated growth and recruited more tumor-reactive CD8 T cells though these cells were dysfunctional. Importantly, PD-1 blockade treatment significantly slowed tumor progression and restored T cell functionality in ISG15-expressing tumors. Our findings indicate that chronic ISG15 exposure promotes CD8 T cell dysfunction; however, these cells remain responsive to PD-1 blockade. This study reveals ISG15 as a potential biomarker for identifying patients likely to benefit from PD-1 blockade therapy.","gpl":"11154","gse":"297506","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/08/10","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8993611","title":"Day28, ISG15hi, Replicate1"},{"accession":"GSM8993614","title":"Day28, ISG15hi, Replicate2"},{"accession":"GSM8993609","title":"Day28,ChronicTCR"},{"accession":"GSM8993612","title":"Day28, Dysf+ISG15hi, Replicate1"},{"accession":"GSM8993615","title":"Day28, Dysf+ISG15hi, Replicate2"},{"accession":"GSM8993610","title":"Day28,Dysf,Replicate1"},{"accession":"GSM8993613","title":"Day28,Dysf,Replicate2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":7,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40879086"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE297nnn/GSE297506/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1265105"},"200287301":{"uid":"200287301","accession":"GSE287301","gds":"","title":"Single-cell RNA-sequencing of HNSCC-infiltrating T cells","summary":"This study examines the T cell landscape in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) using single-cell RNA sequencing combined with T cell receptor and protein profiling. By analyzing tumor-infiltrating T cells from 28 patients, we identified diverse T cell subsets, characterized their transcriptional states, and mapped their clonal dynamics. These findings provide a detailed view of the immune microenvironment in HNSCC and offer insights into T cell-mediated immunity and potential targets for immunotherapy and are patient-matched to PBMC TCR sequencing and Xenium spatial transcriptomics in separate GEO submissions.","gpl":"34284","gse":"287301","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/08/06","suppfile":"CLOUPE, CSV, HTML, TAR, TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8743465","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 8"},{"accession":"GSM8743445","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743482","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743485","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743442","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743488","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743462","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743474","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743468","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743448","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743451","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 2"},{"accession":"GSM8743454","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743471","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743447","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743467","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 2"},{"accession":"GSM8743470","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743487","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743473","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 16"},{"accession":"GSM8743450","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743476","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743479","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743456","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743455","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743459","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 2"},{"accession":"GSM8743452","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743484","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743453","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743464","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743458","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743461","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743478","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743444","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743481","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 8"},{"accession":"GSM8743475","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 2"},{"accession":"GSM8743483","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 2"},{"accession":"GSM8743477","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743472","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743457","title":"Gene expression: chip 2 pool 16"},{"accession":"GSM8743460","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 3"},{"accession":"GSM8743480","title":"TCR-seq: chip 1 pool 7"},{"accession":"GSM8743446","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743486","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 5"},{"accession":"GSM8743449","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 8"},{"accession":"GSM8743463","title":"CITE-seq: chip 1 pool 6"},{"accession":"GSM8743489","title":"TCR-seq: chip 2 pool 16"},{"accession":"GSM8743466","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 1"},{"accession":"GSM8743469","title":"CITE-seq: chip 2 pool 4"},{"accession":"GSM8743443","title":"Gene expression: chip 1 pool 2"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":48,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["41961948"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE287nnn/GSE287301/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1212073"},"200273127":{"uid":"200273127","accession":"GSE273127","gds":"","title":"Comparative single-cell analysis of esophageal cancer subtypes reveals cellular distinctions explaining varied immunotherapy responses","summary":"Esophageal cancer comprises two main subtypes: esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) and esophageal adenocarcinoma (EAC). Previous studies revealed their distinct genomic characteristics, sharing similarities with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) and gastric adenocarcinoma (GAC) for ESCC and EAC, respectively. This study compares ESCC and EAC at single-cell resolution to identify factors explaining differences in immunotherapy response rates. We performed comparative single-cell transcriptome analysis for four cancer types near the esophagus using data from 35 patients with ESCC, EAC, HNSCC, or GAC. Malignant epithelial cells showed distinct separations between subtypes based on histological origins. We identified enrichment of CXCL13+ CD8+ T cells and CXCL9+CXCL10+ TAMs in ESCC and HNSCC, promoting a hot-tumor environment through interferon-γ pathways. Conversely, hypoxia and cold-tumor related populations, such as heat-shock protein high CD8+ T cells and MARCO+ TAMs, were enriched in EAC and GAC. These immune subsets' expression signatures predicted immunotherapy responses across diverse cancer types.","gpl":"20795","gse":"273127","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/08/01","suppfile":"H5","samples":[{"accession":"GSM8422226","title":"EC06, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422229","title":"EC09, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422223","title":"EC03, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422224","title":"EC04, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422221","title":"EC01, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422227","title":"EC07, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422230","title":"EC11, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422222","title":"EC02, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422231","title":"EC12, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422225","title":"EC05, scRNA-seq"},{"accession":"GSM8422228","title":"EC08, scRNA-seq"}],"relations":[],"extrelations":[],"n_samples":11,"seriestitle":"","platformtitle":"","platformtaxa":"","samplestaxa":"","pubmedids":["40581834"],"projects":[],"ftplink":"ftp://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/series/GSE273nnn/GSE273127/","geo2r":"no","bioproject":"PRJNA1140221"},"200304200":{"uid":"200304200","accession":"GSE304200","gds":"","title":"Bulk RNA sequencing of de novo and radiation-recurrent head and neck squamous cell carcinoma tumors","summary":"We performed bulk RNA sequencing on head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC) tumors to identify, characterize, and select specifc cancer-testis antigens (CTAs) as potential precision immuno-oncologic targets. Specifically, we compared CTA expression between previously untreated (de novo) tumors and radiation-recurrent tumors to assess expression patterns in different clinical settings.","gpl":"24676","gse":"304200","taxon":"Homo sapiens","entrytype":"GSE","gdstype":"Expression profiling by high throughput sequencing","ptechtype":"","valtype":"","ssinfo":"","subsetinfo":"","pdat":"2025/07/31","suppfile":"TXT","samples":[{"accession":"GSM9144953","title":"P018 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144956","title":"P022 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144950","title":"P015 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144967","title":"P034 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144964","title":"P031 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144944","title":"P009 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144947","title":"P012 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144968","title":"P035 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144945","title":"P010 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144951","title":"P016 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144948","title":"P013 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144959","title":"P026 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144942","title":"P007 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144962","title":"P029 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144965","title":"P032 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144939","title":"P004 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144940","title":"P005 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GSM9144943","title":"P008 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144963","title":"P030 tumor, de novo"},{"accession":"GSM9144946","title":"P011 tumor, radiation-recurrent"},{"accession":"GS